+  // KSX H1 (local Kapitsa patch 0014, SPLASH_KSX_H1=1; stock never uses it): the step stays inside the command
+  // buffer. The dispatch right after it (ksx_h1_step) writes EventStep::ksxStep into ksxCtl[0] once the work before
+  // it completed and spins until the runner, after `work`, writes it into ksxCtl[1]; on its spin bound it writes it
+  // into ksxCtl[2] and goes on, and the command then fails and marks the backend unhealthy.
+  InBuffer,
+  // KSX H1 with the event-path fallback (SPLASH_KSX_H1=2): the dispatch right before the step (ksx_h1_step) writes
+  // ksxStep into ksxCtl[0] and spins until ksxCtl[1] reaches it, on its bound counting into ksxCtl[3] and going on;
+  // the step then ends the buffer, and the next one waits on the runner's event as an idle step's does, so a spin
+  // that gave up costs time, not correctness. The runner picks the step up from ksxCtl[0].
+  InBufferEvent,
+  // KSX H1 gate and replay (SPLASH_KSX_H1=3): as InBuffer, but ksx_h1_step runs as ksx_h1_gate_step, and every
+  // dispatch after the command's first such step reads its grid from the command's gate table (or its own indirect
+  // grid, which must be GPU-written). On its spin bound the step writes its number into ksxCtl[2], skips its copy-in,
+  // zeroes the gate slots of every later dispatch and the indirect grids they read (saved first), and the runner
+  // skips every later host step; once the command completed, the backend restores the grids and encodes the command
+  // again from that ksx_h1_step on, its later in-buffer steps as awake event steps. A step runs in-buffer only when
+  // the command holds its graph (Command::keepAlive), signals no other agent after it, and is not its first host
+  // step; otherwise it runs as an awake event step.
+  InBufferGate,
 };
 
